\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Current divergence on loops and parallel bonds}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{For the [massive graded Wilson data](fdip-00IN), take a labeled positive edge #{e=(u,j):u\to v=u+e_j}, and write #{h_e=U_j(u)^q}. The directional spin endomorphisms are
##{A_j=\frac{\gamma_j}{2a}-\frac{c_W}{a^2}I_S,\qquad
B_j=-\frac{\gamma_j}{2a}-\frac{c_W}{a^2}I_S.}
With #{\mu=m+2dc_W/a^2} and the canonical identifications of the site fibers, direct substitution of the shifts gives
##{\bar\psi(K_U\psi)=\mu\sum_x\bar\psi_x(\psi_x)
+\sum_{e=(u,j)}\left(\bar\psi_u(A_jh_e^{-1}\psi_v)+\bar\psi_v(B_jh_e\psi_u)\right).}
For this bond the current appearing in \ref{fdip-00J2} is
##{j_e=\bar\psi_u(A_jh_e^{-1}\psi_v)-\bar\psi_v(B_jh_e\psi_u).}
Acting at the endpoints, or differentiating #{h_e=\ue^{iq\varphi_e}}, gives
##{Q_z=\sum_{\operatorname{head}(e)=z}j_e-\sum_{\operatorname{tail}(e)=z}j_e,
\qquad \partial_{\varphi_e}(-\bar\psi(K_U\psi))=iqj_e.}
A loop occurs once in each divergence sum and cancels, although its individual angular derivative may be nonzero. For period two, both distinct positive bonds contribute separately. Using the aggregate operator between two site fibers as the contribution of each bond would count those terms twice. The labeled-bond calculation gives incoming-minus-outgoing divergence for every positive period. These are finite coefficient identities; a continuum anomaly or renormalized Ward-Takahashi statement requires additional hypotheses.}
